    Caption = 'A seal belongs to the closed, packed mailer, not to an empty carton drawing.'
    Published = '2026-09-30'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can a decorative sticker replace shipping tape?', 'Only if the pack construction and trial show it can do the required closure job. Do not treat a printed branding seal as a universal replacement for structural tape.'),
      @('What size should a mailer seal be?', 'Close a packed mailer first, measure the flap overlap and usable flat areas, then test a sample across the actual seam.'),
      @('Are all mailer seal stickers tamper evident?', 'No. A regular closure sticker and a tamper-evident construction serve different purposes. Request a specific sample and documented behavior if tamper evidence is required.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>At an e-commerce packing table, the last minute is usually busy: tissue goes in, a product card slides under the lid and somebody presses the flap down. A round branded sticker is easy to admire in a mockup. The more useful question is whether it can bridge the closed mailer without lifting, slowing the team or tearing the box in an ugly way when the customer opens it.</p>
  <p>I would not start with the sticker diameter. I would start with a packed mailer. The flap overlap and the paper finish decide how much label can sit on each side of the seam. A seal that looks centered on an empty box can become a narrow bridge once the carton is full.</p>
  <h2>Decide Whether the Sticker Is Structural or Decorative</h2>
  <p>A small printed seal can signal that a package has not been casually opened and make unboxing feel deliberate. It should not be assumed to replace tape, locking tabs or a tested closure. If the shipping box relies on the sticker to remain shut, that is a stronger performance requirement and needs a pack-out trial through handling and transit conditions.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Question to answer</th><th>Evidence to gather</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Decorative finish</td><td>Does the printed mark look clear?</td><td>Final-size proof on the mailer</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Flap closure</td><td>Does the seam stay closed?</td><td>Packed-box application test</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tamper evidence</td><td>What must happen on removal?</td><td>Specific material sample and review</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Customer opening</td><td>Can the box open cleanly?</td><td>One complete unboxing trial</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fast packing</td><td>Can staff peel and place it easily?</td><td>Trial in the normal packing flow</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <h2>Test Paper Dust and Surface Coatings</h2>
  <p>Kraft board can be textured or dusty. A glossy printed mailer may have a coating that changes label contact. Ask for a sample of the actual carton, not a generic color swatch. Apply a small set where the packing team will put them, with the same pressure and timing. The first hour on a clean desk says less than the normal packing routine.</p>
  <p>Open the sealed mailer as a customer would. A label that tears the top layer of a gift box may be the wrong experience even if it holds well. Conversely, a label that releases neatly might be unsuitable when the project explicitly needs visible interference. Define the outcome before choosing an adhesive or security feature.</p>
  <h2>Give the Branding a Purpose</h2>
  <p>An original monogram, tiny parcel illustration or simple color device can make the seal belong to the brand. Blank circles are not a safer design strategy; they merely remove recognition. Keep the key mark away from the fold line, where it can distort, and judge the artwork at the actual seal size. If a QR code is included, test it after application and bending, not just on a flat file.</p>
  <h2>Write a Brief the Packing Team Can Use</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Close a mailer packed with its usual contents</li><li>Measure flap overlap and flat contact area in mm</li><li>Identify carton stock and coating</li><li>State whether the sticker decorates, closes or evidences opening</li><li>Test peeling and placement during normal packing</li><li>Open the package and inspect the customer experience</li><li>Confirm quantities, roll or sheet format and artwork version</li></ul>
  <p>Read the <a href="/blog/tamper-evident-labels-guide/">tamper-evident label guide</a> if visible interference is a genuine requirement; the <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-packaging-and-packing-options/">packing options guide</a> covers supply formats. Add a packed-box photo and intended seal job to the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. A closure sticker earns its place when the parcel arrives intact and still feels good to open.</p>
